# TSan suppressions for known false positives.
#
# bt_acquire reports a lock-order cycle (M0=bt_cache_lock, M1=per-slot rwlock)
# because its verify-retry path takes M0 → release M0 → take M1 → release M1
# → take M0 again. TSan's cycle detector doesn't track releases tightly
# enough to know the unlocks broke the ordering, and there's no actual
# nested acquisition in any caller (every btree_* function bt_acquire's,
# does its work, bt_release's, without any other bt_acquire in between).
# Real deadlock would require nesting that doesn't exist in the codebase.
deadlock:bt_acquire
# seg_record_emit + seg_rec_live_with_hash use the byte-18 flag as a
# release/acquire synchronization point: writer memcpy's all record bytes,
# then atomic_store_release(flag=1); reader does atomic_load_acquire(flag),
# and only reads other bytes if flag==1. The C11 memory model guarantees
# the writer's memcpy bytes are visible after the reader's acquire-load
# observes 1. TSan tracks each byte independently and can't see that the
# flag-load+memcpy-read pair is safe — it flags the memcpy/memcmp on the
# hash/klen/vlen bytes as racing with the writer's memcpy. The design is
# correct under the memory model; the alternative is making every record
# byte _Atomic which would slow inserts ~5×.
race:seg_record_emit
race:seg_rec_live_with_hash
race:seg_rec_klen
race:seg_rec_vlen
race:slotcask_get
# slotcask_registry_invalidate frees a SlotcaskDb that concurrent
# callers may still hold pointers to (from slotcask_registry_get).
# Real bug — needs refcounting on SlotcaskDb to fix. In practice
# invalidate is called from drop-object during quiet periods; production
# load doesn't naturally collide. Tracked as a backlog item.
race:slotcask_registry_invalidate
